# +Point, +Template, -Test
# +Point, +Template, -Program, -Expected
# THE CLAIMS OF THE HARD CORPUS: for every
# point, the test programs published with it, each `scored` (lambda beta
# plus T = \x. x is held consistent) or `trap` (it is held not to be),
# with its PROOF, `proved(Class)`, whose argument is the class's below
# applied to the construction named at the point's fact, or
# `conjecture(Class)`, a claim believed and not proved, scored as usual
# and open to a refutation.
#
# THE TEMPLATES. A claim names its test by a TEMPLATE; `claim_test/3`
# builds the test program, one surface lambda term, over the point's
# term M, the surface term its `contest_hard` predicate gives. With y, y1,
# y2 free and a the argument the judge applies the test to (the witness):
#
#     boolean(true)             \a. M a y
#     boolean(false)            \a. M y a
#     natural(Count)            \a. test(Count, M, a), where
#                               test(0, N, a) = N a y and
#                               test(K + 1, N, a) = N y (\p. test(K, p, a))
#     member(Index, Certificate)
#                               \a. M (\i. \w. Selected), Selected the
#                               member Index of Certificate applied so
#                               that it returns a: member(1, natural) is
#                               i a (the identity member), member(2,
#                               natural) is w y1 y2 a (\a. \b. \c. c),
#                               member(2, stream) is w y1 a y2
#                               (\a. \b. \c. b)
#
# The encodings are the prelude's: true = \t. \f. t, false = \t. \f. f,
# zero = \z. \s. z, successor n = \z. \s. s n; the certificates are
# H22's (`contest_hard_clauses.pl`): natural = \sel. sel (\x. x) (\a. \b.
# \c. c), stream = \sel. sel (\x. bad) (\a. \b. \c. b).
#
# FINITE HEAD, proved. M reduces to the intended value V (a finite
# derivation, certified by a least-solution reading or by the
# argument at the fact). The scored test is V's template: it reduces to
# \a. a (for a numeral, each level applies the successor branch to the
# predecessor, and zero's branch returns a), so T = \x. x by beta, and
# lambda beta is consistent (Church-Rosser). The traps are the other
# boolean, or the numerals one above and one below: they reduce to
# \a. y (true's test on false, false's on true, and the numeral one
# above, whose last level meets zero's first branch) or to
# \a. y (\z. \s. z) (the numeral one below, whose last level meets a
# successor under zero's test), beta eta normal forms distinct from
# \x. x, so T = \x. x is refuted by Boehm's theorem, whose separation
# uses beta alone and so refutes it in lambda beta.
#
# SCORED FINITE HEAD, proved (`scored_finite_head/3`). A finite head as above, but the point poses
# its scored templates only, each proved by the argument at the fact, and
# no trap. A point several of whose values are intended uses it, one
# scored template each: a
# trie union whose pairwise sum is {0, 1, 2} poses natural(0), natural(1)
# and natural(2), and its self-equality both booleans.
#
# NO YIELD, proved (`no_yield_trap/3`, a point with merges). Each
# template's test yields nothing: by the trap pattern of the extension
# with records, every term reachable from the test is missing in
# head or has a merge in head position, shown with a per-point set
# of states in the document the argument names; the judgment is the empty
# disjunction, false. Every template is a trap; the point has no scored
# claim.
#
# NO YIELD, conjectured (`no_yield_conjecture/3`, a point in lambda with
# sets). Each template's test is held to yield nothing, for the reason
# written at the point, which is not proved: every template is a trap, a
# CONJECTURE. A proof moves the point to
# `no_yield_trap/3`.
#
# CYCLE. M is a fixed point: M = C[M] by beta, C the point's
# construction, which applies M's value.
#
# - BOTH BOOLEANS (`or c false`, `and c true`): C[true] = true and
#   C[false] = false by beta. Scored: both boolean tests, CONJECTURES:
#   that M = true (or M = false) is consistent with lambda beta is
#   not proved (M is unsolvable; not every unsolvable term is easy).
# - FALSE ONLY (H15 `and c false`, H29, H30): C[false] = false, and
#   C[true] is a normal form distinct from true (false for H15 and H29,
#   \p. \q. true or \p. \q. \r. \s. true for H30). Scored: boolean(false),
#   a CONJECTURE as above, except on H29's and H30's five points, where
#   it is PROVED (`conjecture_proved/3`), as is H15's. Trap:
#   boolean(true), PROVED, with no eta: the test's closed equation
#   \y. \a. M a y = \y. \a. a, applied to P and Q, gives M P Q = P for
#   all closed P and Q. With it C[M] reduces to a closed normal form N
#   other than true: M false false = false for H15 and H29's and_false,
#   M false M = false for if_false, M true M = true and then
#   true false false = false for and_or, M A false = A for H30 (A is
#   \p. \q. true for the two cycle, \p. \q. \r. \s. true for the three
#   cycle). The fixed point gives M = N, and the closed equation becomes,
#   by beta, \y. \a. N a y = \y. \a. a, whose sides are distinct beta
#   normal forms (\y. \a. y; for H30 \y. \a. true and
#   \y. \a. \r. \s. true), refuted by Boehm's theorem.
# - CERTIFICATE MEMBER (H22 symbol, H24 pair): assumed for the judgment,
#   the natural certificate is given back. Scored: member(1, natural),
#   member(2, natural) and member(2, stream), all PROVED
#   (certificate_collapse, `gezi/doc/proofs/certificate_ghost_solutions.md`,
#   2026-09-28): for every closed normal W, lambda beta
#   eta plus M = \sel. sel (\x. x) W is consistent, and the natural and
#   the stream certificate are two such values; the judgment reads only
#   the first member of what it gives back.
# - REJECTS CERTIFICATE (H22's twin): the judgment satisfies
#   J T = (J T) (\i. \w. w). Traps: member(2, natural) and member(2,
#   stream), CONJECTURES: a certificate would equal its own second member,
#   which neither does; a proof from the member tests alone is not
#   written.
#
# A cycle that hands its value on without applying it is solved by every
# closed abstraction; such points carry no claim and are retired
# (`contest_hard_answers.pl`). Points not listed here have no claims and
# are not scored as claims.

